Here's a simple solution in Java. First, we'll need a method to check if a number is prime, then we'll use Java's Stream API to filter the list:

```java
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> numbers = Arrays.asList(2, 7, 8, 10, 11, 15, 71);
        List<Integer> primes = numbers.stream()
                                      .filter(Main::isPrime)
                                      .collect(Collectors.toList());
        System.out.println(primes);
    }

    private static boolean isPrime(int number) {
        if (number <= 1) {
            return false;
        }
        for (int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(number); i++) {
            if (number % i == 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

When you run this program, it prints `[2, 7, 11, 71]`, which are the prime numbers in the list. The `isPrime` method checks for primality by checking divisibility of all numbers from 2 to the square root of the given number - this is an optimization, as any factor of the number must be less than or equal to its square root.

Note: for large lists, a more efficient algorithm for checking primality may be needed because this approach can become quite slow.

```php
function validateFormInput($input, $rules) {
    $errors = [];

    foreach ($rules as $field => $rule) {
        $rulesArray = explode('|', $rule);
        foreach ($rulesArray as $singleRule) {
            if ($singleRule === 'required' && empty($input[$field])) {
                $errors[$field][] = "The $field field is required.";
            } elseif ($singleRule === 'alpha' && !ctype_alpha($input[$field])) {
                $errors[$field][] = "The $field field must contain only alphabetic characters.";
            } elseif (strpos($singleRule, 'max:') === 0) {
                $maxLength = (int) substr($singleRule, 4);
                if (strlen($input[$field]) > $maxLength) {
                    $errors[$field][] = "The $field field must not exceed $maxLength characters in length.";
                }
            }
        }
    }

    return $errors;
}

// Example usage
$input = [
    'name' => 'John123'
];

$rules = [
    'name' => 'required|alpha|max:50'
];

$errors = validateFormInput($input, $rules);
print_r($errors);
// Output: Array ( [name] => Array ( [0] => The name field must contain only alphabetic characters. ) )
```

