How can I figure out the size of a file, in bytes?


#include <stdio.h>

unsigned int fsize(char* file){
  //what goes here?
}

To determine the size of the file, you can use the fseek() and ftell() functions provided by the C standard library. Here's how to do it:

```C
#include <stdio.h>

unsigned long fsize(char* file) {
  FILE *f = fopen(file, "r");
  if (f == NULL) {
    printf("Failed to open file\n");
    return -1;
  }
  fseek(f, 0, SEEK_END);
  unsigned long len = (unsigned long)ftell(f);
  fclose(f);
  return len;
}
```
In the code, you open the file in read mode, move the file pointer to the end using fseek and then use ftell to tell the current position of the file pointer. This position will effectively be the size of the file. Be sure to close the file using fclose when you are done.

Please note the return value of this function is unsigned long. If the size of file is expected to be longer than unsigned int, it is safer to use long to avoid overflow.

