&lt;p&gt;Adam engages in a unique game involving his birthday cake.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;He slices a portion, creating a circular sector of $60$ degrees, and inverts the piece, positioning the icing at the bottom.&lt;br&gt;
Subsequently, he rotates the cake by $60$ degrees in the counterclockwise direction, cuts an adjacent $60$ degree piece, and flips it upside down.&lt;br&gt;
This process is repeated until, after a total of twelve steps, all the icing is restored to the top.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Intriguingly, this method is effective for any piece size, even if the cutting angle is an irrational number: all the icing will eventually return to the top after a finite number of steps.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Adam then experiments with a different approach: he alternates cutting pieces of size $x=\frac{360}{9}$ degrees, $y=\frac{360}{10}$ degrees and $z=\frac{360 }{\sqrt{11}}$ degrees. The first piece he cuts is of size $x$ and he flips it. The second is of size $y$ and he flips it. The third is of size $z$ and he flips it. He continues this pattern with pieces of size $x$, $y$ and $z$ in that sequence until all the icing is back on top, and he finds that he requires $60$ flips in total.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div align=&#34;center&#34;&gt;&lt;img src=&#34;resources/images/0566-cakeicingpuzzle.gif?1678992057&#34; alt=&#34;0566-cakeicingpuzzle.gif&#34;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's define $F(a, b, c)$ as the least number of piece flips required to bring all the icing back on top for pieces of size $x=\frac{360}{a}$ degrees, $y=\frac{360}{b}$ degrees and $z=\frac{360}{\sqrt{c}}$ degrees.&lt;br&gt;
Let's also define $G(n) = \sum_{9 \le a \lt b \lt c \le n} F(a,b,c)$, for integers $a$, $b$ and $c$.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is provided that $F(9, 10, 11) = 60$, $F(10, 14, 16) = 506$, $F(15, 16, 17) = 785232$.&lt;br&gt;
Additionally, $G(11) = 60$, $G(14) = 58020$ and $G(17) = 1269260$ are also given.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your task is to determine the value of $G(53)$.&lt;/p&gt;

