Additionally, to efficiently handle large inputs in terms of speed and resource, Python's built-in lru_cache (Least Recently Used Cache) can be used. This is a simple form of memoization, where function calls with previously calculated output values will not be calculated again and instead retrieves the results from the 'cache of function results'.

The input validation needs to be performed in the function to ensure only non-negative integers are provided. This can be done with a simple if statement to check if the input is less than 0. If this is the case, an appropriate exception (in this case `ValueError`) should be thrown.

