Here is the Java program using recursive way:

```java
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 10; // change this value to get more or fewer Fibonacci numbers
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            System.out.print(fibonacci(i) + " ");
        }
    }

    public static int fibonacci(int n) {
        if (n == 0) {
            return 0;
        } else if (n == 1) {
            return 1;
        } else {
            return fibonacci(n - 1) + fibonacci(n - 2);
        }
    }
}
```
In this program, the `fibonacci` method is recursively calling itself to calculate the Fibonacci number at a given position n. This approach may be inefficient for larger values of n because it performs a lot of duplicate calculations.

Now let's see the iterative approach:

```java
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 10; // change this value to get more or fewer Fibonacci numbers
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            System.out.print(fibonacci(i) + " ");
        }
    }

    public static int fibonacci(int n) {
        int a = 0, b = 1, c;
        if (n == 0)
            return a;
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            c = a + b;
            a = b;
            b = c;
        }
        return b;
    }
}
```
This program uses the iterative approach to calculate the Fibonacci sequence. It involves fewer duplicate calculations compared to the recursive method. The `fibonacci` function changes the values of a and b with each iteration, reducing the need to calculate the same values multiple times.

