You are tasked with implementing a custom immutable stack data structure in C#. The stack should support the standard push, pop, and peek operations, and it should be implemented using an underlying immutable collection provided by the `System.Collections.Immutable` namespace.

Your task is to complete the implementation of the `ImmutableStack` class, which should have the following methods:
- `Push`: Adds an element to the top of the stack and returns a new stack with the element added.
- `Pop`: Removes the element at the top of the stack and returns a new stack with the element removed.
- `Peek`: Returns the element at the top of the stack without removing it.

You should use the `ImmutableStack<T>` class provided by the `System.Collections.Immutable` namespace to implement the custom immutable stack.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Immutable;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        var stack = ImmutableStack<int>.Empty;
        stack = stack.Push(1);
        stack = stack.Push(2);
        stack = stack.Push(3);

        Console.WriteLine(stack.Peek());  // Output: 3
        stack = stack.Pop();
        Console.WriteLine(stack.Peek());  // Output: 2
    }
}
```

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Immutable;

public class ImmutableStack<T>
{
    private ImmutableStack<T> previousStack;
    private T value;

    private ImmutableStack(ImmutableStack<T> previousStack, T value)
    {
        this.previousStack = previousStack;
        this.value = value;
    }

    public ImmutableStack<T> Push(T value)
    {
        return new ImmutableStack<T>(this, value);
    }

    public ImmutableStack<T> Pop()
    {
        if (previousStack == null)
        {
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Cannot pop from an empty stack");
        }
        return previousStack;
    }

    public T Peek()
    {
        if (previousStack == null)
        {
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Cannot peek an empty stack");
        }
        return value;
    }
}
```

