This problem can be solved by using a Hash Set to store all the possible form of the words by modifying each character at each position, and removing one character or adding one character at each position of word.

Here is the Java code for your problem.

```java
class MagicDictionary {
    Set<String> set;

    /** Initialize your data structure here. */
    public MagicDictionary() {
        set = new HashSet<>();
    }

    public void buildDict(String[] dictionary) {
        for (String word : dictionary) {
            for (int i = 0; i < word.length(); i++) {
                // modify each character at position i
                for (char c = 'a'; c <= 'z'; c++) {
                    if (c != word.charAt(i)) {
                        String newWord = word.substring(0, i) + c + word.substring(i + 1);
                        set.add(newWord);
                    }
                }
                // remove character at position i
                String removedWord = word.substring(0, i) + word.substring(i + 1);
                set.add(removedWord);

            }
            // add one character at each position
            for (int i = 0; i <= word.length(); i++) {
              for (char c = 'a'; c <= 'z'; c++) {
                String addedWord = word.substring(0, i) + c + word.substring(i);
                set.add(addedWord);
              }
            }
        }
    }


    public boolean search(String searchWord) {
        return set.contains(searchWord);
    }
}

/**
 * Your MagicDictionary object will be instantiated and called as such:
 * MagicDictionary obj = new MagicDictionary();
 * obj.buildDict(dictionary);
 * boolean param_2 = obj.search(searchWord);
 */
```
With this code, when you build the dictionary, it will generate all possible variations of the words by changing, adding or removing exactly one character and store them in the Hash Set. Later when you search a word, you just need to check if the word exists in the set. This will be done in O(1) time complexity. 

The total time complexity of the whole process is O(n*m^2), where n is the number of words in the dictionary, and m is the length of the longest word in the dictionary. This is because for each word, we need to generate m variations by changing a character and m variations by removing a character, and (m+1) variations by adding a character. Inserting an element into a set takes constant time. So the total time complexity is linear with respect to the total number of characters in all words.

