Here is an example of a database schema in SQL that can store user login credentials securely:

CREATE TABLE users (
  user_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
  username VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,
  password VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
  created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
);

-- Indexes and Constraints
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX unique_username ON users (username);
ALTER TABLE users ADD CONSTRAINT password_length CHECK(length(password) >= 8);

-- Security Measures
GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON users TO authorized_user;
REVOKE SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON users FROM unauthorized_user;
GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION crypt(password TEXT, salt TEXT) TO authorized_user;

In this schema, we have a "users" table that has columns for user ID, username, password, and creation timestamp. The username and password fields are VARCHAR types, which means they can store up to a certain number of characters.

We also have two indexes and a constraint in place. The "unique_username" index ensures that there are no duplicate usernames in the table. The "password_length" constraint requires that all passwords are at least 8 characters long. This helps to increase security by preventing weak passwords from being used.

To implement adequate security measures, we grant SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE permissions on the "users" table to authorized users only. We also revoke these permissions from any unauthorized users. Finally, we grant EXECUTE permissions on the crypt() function to authorized users. This function is used to hash passwords before they are stored in the database, helping to prevent unauthorized access or theft of sensitive data.

