No, there isn't a specific language construct in C# to get the current iteration of a foreach loop. The way you're doing it (incrementing a counter) is the idiomatic way in C#.

Additionally, the foreach construct is designed specifically for iterating over collections without worrying about the element index, because it's not always meaningful. If you need the index, you may want to consider whether a for loop might be more appropriate.

Alternatively, If you're working with LINQ, you can use the Select method with an overload that provides the index of the current element. Here is an example:

```csharp
foreach (var item in collection.Select((value, index) => new {value, index}))
{
    Console.WriteLine($"Value: {item.value} at index: {item.index}");
}
```
In this code, Select is transforming each item in the collection into a new anonymous type that includes the value and its index. This allows you to keep tracking of index alongside with value in foreach loop which is more LINQ-idiomatic way. But remember this will cause an extra memory overhead for creating new objects for each item in the collection.

