```c
#include<stdio.h>

void min_max(float a, float b, float c, float* min, float* max){
    *min = a;
    *max = a;
    if(b < *min)
        *min = b;
    if(c < *min)
        *min = c;
    if(b > *max)
        *max = b;
    if(c > *max)
        *max = c;
}

int main(){
    float a, b, c; // Input numbers
    float w1, w2, w3; // Weights
    float weighted_avg;
    float min, max;

    printf("Enter three numbers: ");
    scanf("%f %f %f", &a, &b, &c);

    printf("Enter weights for the three numbers: ");
    scanf("%f %f %f", &w1, &w2, &w3);

    if(w1 < 0 || w2 < 0 || w3 < 0){
        printf("Error: Negative weights are not allowed.\n");
    }
    else if(w1 + w2 + w3 == 0){
        printf("Error: Sum of weights is 0. Cannot find weighted average.\n");
    }
    else {
        weighted_avg = (a*w1 + b*w2 + c*w3) / (w1 + w2 + w3);
        printf("Weighted average: %.02f\n", weighted_avg);
    }

    min_max(a, b, c, &min, &max);
    printf("Minimum: %.02f\n", min);
    printf("Maximum: %.02f\n", max);

    return 0;
}
```

This program does the following:

1. Asks the user for three numbers and corresponding weights
2. Checks for any invalid inputs (negative weights, sum of weights equal to 0)
3. Calculates and displays the weighted average if inputs are valid
4. Finds the minimum and maximum of the three numbers using a `min_max` function

Please note that this program does not handle erroneous user input other than negative weights and zero sum of weights. You can use additional input validation if required for your use case.

