\[\dfrac{1}{2} 왼쪽( \dfrac{17\pi}{60} + \dfrac{37\pi}{60}\right) = \dfrac{27 \pi}{60} = \dfrac{9\pi}{20}.\]따라서 \[\dfrac{1\pi}{2\]가 됩니다,
\[ e^{17\pi i/ 60} + e^{37 \pi i/60} = r_2 e^{9 \pi i/20},\]는 음수가 아닌 $r_2$에 대해.

마지막으로, 중간 수는 $e^{27\pi i/60} = e^{9\pi i/20}$로 분수를 단순화합니다. 이제 인수가 $e^{9\pi i/20}$인 세 개의 숫자를 더하면 같은 인수를 가진 또 다른 숫자를 얻을 수 있습니다. 더 정확하게 말하면 다음과 같습니다.
\begin{align*}
e^{7\pi i/60} + e^{17\pi i/60} + e^{27 \pi i/60} + e^{37\pi i /60} + e^{47 \pi i /60} &= (e^{7\pi i/60} + e^{47\pi i/60}) + e^{27 \pi i/60} + (e^{37\pi i /60} + e^{47 \pi i /60}) \\
&= r_1 e^{9\pi i/20} + e^{9\pi i/20} + r_2 e^{9\pi i/20} \\
&= (r_1 +r_2 + 1) e^{9\pi i/20},
\end{align*}합계의 인수는 $\dfrac{9\pi}{20}$가 됩니다.

write an equivalent for the following in rust:
def elaborate_merged_list(input_list1: list, input_list2: list):
    merged_list = []
    for element in input_list1:
        if element not in merged_list:
            merged_list.append(element)
    for element in input_list2:
        if element not in merged_list:
            merged_list.append(element)

    # Perform Bubble Sort on merged_list in descending order
    for iteration in range(len(merged_list)):
        for index in range(len(merged_list)-1):
            if merged_list[index] < merged_list[index + 1]:
                merged_list[index], merged_list[index + 1] = merged_list[index + 1], merged_list[index]
    return merged_list

Here is how you would define the function in Rust:

```rust
fn elaborate_merged_list(input_list1: Vec<i32>, input_list2: Vec<i32>) -> Vec<i32> {
    let mut merged_list: Vec<i32> = Vec::new();

    for &element in &input_list1 {
        if !merged_list.contains(&element) {
            merged_list.push(element);
        }
    }
    for &element in &input_list2 {
        if !merged_list.contains(&element) {
            merged_list.push(element);
        }
    }

    // Perform Bubble Sort on merged_list in descending order
    let len = merged_list.len();
    for _ in 0..len {
        for i in 0..len - 1 {
            if merged_list[i] < merged_list[i + 1] {
                merged_list.swap(i, i + 1);
            }
        }
    }

    merged_list
}
```

This does exactly the same thing as the Python version. Important notes:

- Rust doesn't have a built-in function similar to python's 'in' keyword for checking containment. So, we use the .contains() function in Rust to achieve the same result.
- In the bubble sort loop, instead of directly swapping the elements like in Python, we use the .swap() function provided by the Vec type in Rust.
- The type of the elements in the list is i32, a 32 bit integer, but you can change this to any type you want as long as it is comparable (i.e., it implements the PartialEq and PartialOrd traits).
- As there's no type like python's list in Rust, we use Vec<i32> which stands for a dynamic or 'growable' array of i32. It's important to note that this array has a homogenous data type (i.e., all elements must be of the same type).
- The "-> Vec<i32>" is the Rust syntax for declaring the return type of a function. All Rust functions declare their return types explicitly.

