public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] arr = {"apple", "banana", "cherry", "apple", "banana", "apple"};
        Map<String, List<Integer>> res = findIndexes(arr);
        System.out.println(res.get("apple"));  //prints: [0, 3, 5]
    }

    public static Map<String, List<Integer>> findIndexes(String[] arr) {
        Map<String, List<Integer>> map = new HashMap<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            if (map.containsKey(arr[i])) {
                List<Integer> indexes = map.get(arr[i]);
                indexes.add(i);
                map.put(arr[i], indexes);
            } else {
                ArrayList<Integer> indexes = new ArrayList<>();
                indexes.add(i);
                map.put(arr[i], indexes);
            }
        }
        return map;
    }
}
```

Here is what the method does:
1. It initializes an empty hash map.
2. It iterates through the array of strings. During each iteration, it checks whether the hash map already contains the current string. 
3. If the string is not in the hash map, it adds the string as a key and creates a new ArrayList containing the current index.
4. If the string is already in the map, it gets the ArrayList associated with the string, appends the current index to the list, and updates the map with the new list.
5. At the end of the function, each key in the map is a string from the array, and its value is a list of indexes where the string can be found.

