This code has a few problems, but I just wanted to give you an idea of what I want.

It's a great idea to structure your GUI in such a way that everything is contained within one primary class. It allows you to efficiently manage and control all of the aspects of the GUI, such as drawing and handling events. From your code, one big issue is that you're using a hardcoded array to store the buttons. This will restrict you heavily as you may want a few or a lot of buttons, and "10" may not always be the right amount.

For the design of your Gui class, one suggestion would be replacing your plain array with a dynamic data structure like `std::vector<>` which can grow and shrink at runtime.

```cpp
#include <vector>

class Gui {
    public:
        void update_all();
        void draw_all() const;

        void addButton(Button* button) { buttons.push_back(button); }  // Just adds to end of vector
        void removeButton(Button* button) { buttons.erase(std::remove(buttons.begin(), buttons.end(), button), buttons.end()); } // Remove specific button

    private:
        std::vector<Button*> buttons; // No need for num_buttons, just use buttons.size()
};
```

This code will give you much more flexibility. Remember that raw pointers in C++ can be tricky to handle due to memory management and it may be beneficial to use smart pointers instead (e.g. `std::unique_ptr<Button>` or `std::shared_ptr<Button>`, depending on your use case).

